  "summary": "PCMag explains how Flock Safety's ALPR and camera network operates, drawing on YouTuber Benn Jordan's research into vulnerabilities, misconfigured cameras, and a Dunwoody, GA incident where a Flock employee accessed a children's gymnastics camera feed. The article also surveys resident pushback nationwide, including litigation in San Jose, vandalism in Oakland, and cancellations in places like Weston, MO.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "He told me he found 70 cameras accessible without a username or password, and browsed the video feeds for a month.",
      "paragraph_idx": 19
    },
    {
      "quote": "Hunyar identified a Flock employee who accessed cameras in a children's gymnastics room at the nearby Jewish community center.",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "In November of 2025, the Electronic Frontier Foundation revealed that police used Flock's ALPR network to track activists and protestors.",
      "paragraph_idx": 26
    },
    {
      "quote": "the city council in Weston, MO, voted this week to remove Flock cameras following significant resident pushback.",
      "paragraph_idx": 28
    },
    {
      "quote": "the city council's decision to renew its contract for nearly 300 ALPRs and to approve the installation of 40 livestream-enabled Flock Condor cameras.",
      "paragraph_idx": 30
    }
  ],
